\paragraph{Beyond manipulation.}
We also evaluate \name{} on the ExoRL RND datasets (\cref{tab:benchmarks}).
Performance is mixed: \name{} achieves the highest return on \texttt{quadruped} and \texttt{point-mass} and the highest mean over the four domains, but trails
on \texttt{walker} and \texttt{cheetah}.
FB leads on \texttt{walker} and \texttt{cheetah}; on \texttt{cheetah}, whose rewards are speed thresholds, \name{} trails all three reported methods.

\section{Conclusion}
\aditya{Update}
% We studied a failure mode of SM approximations in which learned representations lose distinctions between actions at control bottlenecks. 
We introduced \name{}, which applies a softmax to bilinear scores, learns the resulting successor distributions through a categorical Bellman objective, and evaluates actions by integrating rewards under these distributions. 
In controlled bottleneck problems, this parameterization preserves action-value differences with fewer features than FB. 
Across OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill, Density-FB achieves higher mean success than FB on all eleven manipulation tasks. 
